00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.350
So Terrance, why
don't you tell us

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.990
a little about yourself, what
you do, the things you do

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.880
a Google, et cetera, et cetera?

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.800
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
So my job title

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.550
is called technical
solutions engineer.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.250
It is under the cloud
platform support umbrella.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:33.160
And what I basically
do is-- customers

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.700
come to the support team
when they're very anxious

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:36.850
or they have a problem.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.890
And I'm there to help guide
them to a solution that

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:44.439
makes them less anxious
or resolves the issue.

00:05:44.439 --> 00:05:46.480
MARK MANDEL: So, Terrance,
we talked a little bit

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.070
about support.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.590
You said support
a couple of times

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.410
when you were explaining
what it is you do.

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.100
But what is support?

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.490
What does that actually
mean Google Cloud Platform?

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:56.656
BRIAN DORSEY: What's an
example of a kind of problem

00:05:56.656 --> 00:05:58.296
we help with with support?

00:05:58.296 --> 00:06:00.020
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
Good question.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.130
So it really ranges
on the issue.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.782
So we'll help you anything from
any of your billing issues,

00:06:05.782 --> 00:06:08.240
like you want to change the
credit card you're paying with,

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.580
or you're finally at a point
in which your credit card

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.550
limit is less than the bill
you have on Cloud Platform.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.790
So we'll help you get to the
next level of paying via wire

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:23.140
transfer or we call
it offline billing,

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:27.140
where it's not like a credit
card that's charged each week.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.360
We'll help you with bugs you
think might be in the system.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.540
We'll help you get all the
information needed and required

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.736
to report it to engineering.

00:06:36.736 --> 00:06:39.110
We'll work with engineering
to make sure they're actually

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.250
working on the bugs.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.120
So customers who are
running into issues

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.430
don't get that anymore.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:54.440
We work on things prior to even
customers reporting problems.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:59.350
So we look at products
before they even

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:00.420
get in customer hands.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.710
We'll look at the
error messages that

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.430
are going to be generated
and be like, hey,

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:05.640
is that an error message
that a customer can actually

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:10.340
use and figure out
their own problem?

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:13.330
We very much want people to
come and ask us questions.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:14.980
But at the same
time we want people

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.030
to be able to use the product
without having to come to us,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.867
only coming to us when they're
in a very, very odd situation.

00:07:22.867 --> 00:07:23.700
MARK MANDEL: Gotcha.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.530
So you're both helping people
directly and systematically

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:29.190
improving things so that
that help isn't even needed

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:30.079
in the future.

00:07:30.079 --> 00:07:30.620
Is that true?

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:31.620
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.230
Basically.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:32.870
Well--

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:33.990
MARK MANDEL: Sometimes, please.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.823
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: We
want to keep our jobs.

00:07:35.823 --> 00:07:40.030
But at the same time, we
don't want to keep our jobs.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:40.530
All right?

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:42.363
BRIAN DORSEY: So you're
actually kind of try

00:07:42.363 --> 00:07:44.750
to make things better so that
you can move on to deeper

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.041
and deeper things rather than--

00:07:46.041 --> 00:07:47.940
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.850
We don't want to be your
typical support team.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:56.110
We want to be the people that
you partner with and help grow

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.680
your company on our platform.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:01.460
When you have the questions, the
tough, the nitty gritty stuff,

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:06.400
we want to be the team you
come to or you ask advice to.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.060
We don't want to be the team
you go to when it's like, oh,

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.160
I got an error.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.589
We're happy to do
that type of stuff.

00:08:14.589 --> 00:08:16.380
But we don't want you
to have to come to us

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:17.802
for that type of question.

00:08:17.802 --> 00:08:18.760
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.551
BRIAN DORSEY: So that
takes it even further

00:08:20.551 --> 00:08:22.052
than we usually
think about support.

00:08:22.052 --> 00:08:23.051
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:08:23.051 --> 00:08:25.410
We're really not your
average support team.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:29.913
We very much try to be the
best at what we can do.

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:37.175
We spend a lot of time--
well, I think a lot of people

00:08:37.175 --> 00:08:39.299
spend a lot of time
interacting with support teams.

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:42.809
And your cell phone
providers, you

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:45.697
have to go to them because
something's not working right,

00:08:45.697 --> 00:08:47.280
not to knock any
cell phone providers.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:48.654
But sometimes when
you go to them

00:08:48.654 --> 00:08:51.082
they're not always the most
helpful people they can be.

00:08:51.082 --> 00:08:52.540
And that's like
the number one goal

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.970
for us is be the most helpful.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.550
We aren't going to just reject
you or say we can't help you.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.430
We're actually going
to try to answer

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:03.549
whatever the question is as long
as it's within what we can do.

00:09:03.549 --> 00:09:05.340
BRIAN DORSEY: So what
are those parameters?

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.800
Are they broken up into
groups or what can we do?

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:13.310
Is it programmed in tiers or--

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.180
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
So we technically

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.600
support all of the products
under the Google Cloud

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.070
Platform.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:25.850
But sometimes that confuses
some of our customers.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:30.810
Sometimes we get questions
for like, oh, we have-- like,

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.790
oh, I ran out of
quota on the Maps API.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:36.800
Maps API technically
isn't under cloud.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.800
Even though its
an API and it used

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.110
to be in the
developer's console,

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:43.940
we sometimes get
questions for that.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.810
But if you have a question
with Translate API,

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:51.210
that's under the cloud umbrella,
the Vision API app engine.

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.545
Those are all the sorts of
products we really help with.

00:09:53.545 --> 00:09:54.420
BRIAN DORSEY: Gotcha.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:54.710
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:57.490
So if I have a question-- say
I'm writing on Google Cloud

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.540
Platform, like where do I go?

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:05.130
Does it matter if I'm on a
different support package,

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:06.830
or do I all go to
the same place,

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.870
or what avenues are
available to me?

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:16.230
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: So if you're
on one of our higher tiers,

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.680
so silver, gold, or platinum, we
always recommend that you come.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:22.990
You sign into the
Google For Work

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:26.840
Support Center, which is how you
file cases with various support

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:27.800
teams here at Google.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:32.270
It's where you file
support cases with my team.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:36.170
And we always recommend
you come to us first.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:39.800
If you're on the
bronze level, you

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.300
can post your questions on the
Stack Overflow or the Google

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:45.260
Groups public issue trackers.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:49.570
And they're all there and
monitored by our team.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:54.860
But it's not where you can
get one-to-one support.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.340
If you want individual
customized responses

00:10:57.340 --> 00:11:00.650
and questions, the Google
For Work Support Center

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:02.730
is going to your best place.

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.400
But if we see a
question and we think

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.450
it's going to benefit
the community,

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:10.020
we have a whole team of
people that sit and watch

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.380
all of those mediums and
will answer questions

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.550
if it's going to benefit
the entire community.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:16.216
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:11:16.216 --> 00:11:19.060
So going up through the tiers--
you were talking about bronze,

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.150
silver, gold,
platinum-- I believe

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:24.181
everyone is enrolled
in bronze immediately,

00:11:24.181 --> 00:11:26.430
just because you're part of
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:27.670
Is that correct?

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:28.500
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
That is correct.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:28.860
BRIAN DORSEY: Excellent.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:29.360
OK.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:31.880
And that's what gives you--
as soon as you're there,

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.866
like bronze is really-- that's
where you're reaching out

00:11:34.866 --> 00:11:36.740
to support through what
you're talking about.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:37.950
So there's community mediums.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.370
There's developer
communities, Stack Overflow.

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:45.700
And those things are monitored
by the support engineers.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.070
Is that correct?

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:47.600
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
That is correct.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:48.100
Yeah.

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:49.910
We have a whole
team that monitors

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:53.424
all of what we call a one
to many support mediums.

00:11:53.424 --> 00:11:54.840
BRIAN DORSEY: And
then you'll also

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.535
interact with other people
using Google Cloud there, people

00:11:57.535 --> 00:12:01.160
who other customers,
other users,

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:02.790
other people with
similar experience

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:04.541
in those public communities?

00:12:04.541 --> 00:12:05.540
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:09.240
Some of our bigger
communities, such as-- the it's

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.750
called GCE Discuss
or GCE Discussion.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:12.820
It's a Google group.

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:15.980
It's actually really popular
with members of the community

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.000
actually helping each other.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.880
And support actually
doesn't have

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.630
to provide a lot of
help or nurturing

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:23.910
in those types of situations.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.590
And actually, community
is really active there.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:31.100
You'll also find a lot
of our product engineers

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:32.580
on Stack Overflow.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.450
They all follow most
of Stack Overflow.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.560
Most teams that have
a Stack Overflow tag

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.960
also follow along and
provide answers there.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.140
I know the BigQuery team,
you will actually get answers

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:45.607
from real BigQuery engineers.

00:12:45.607 --> 00:12:46.690
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh, awesome.

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:47.740
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Like, if
you have questions there,

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.620
like I don't quite understand
how this function works,

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.080
BigQuery engineers are there.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.710
And they go, hey, yeah.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:54.222
Here's this.

00:12:54.222 --> 00:12:55.930
And then they bring
back the information.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.239
And actually, they improve
their documentation usually.

00:12:58.239 --> 00:12:59.530
BRIAN DORSEY: That's fantastic.

00:12:59.530 --> 00:12:59.740
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:00.782
They answer the question.

00:13:00.782 --> 00:13:01.740
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.850
So the silver, gold,
and platinum, those do

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:05.440
cost extra money, right?

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:06.398
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yes.

00:13:06.398 --> 00:13:09.261
MARK MANDEL: So I see they
all have different benefits

00:13:09.261 --> 00:13:09.760
to those.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.880
But I think I know the
answer to this question.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.210
But I'm going to ask it anyway.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:16.500
What do you think is
the one big reason

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:20.130
for why people should
pay for support

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:21.700
at Google Cloud Platform?

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.880
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
So the reason why

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:28.520
people should pay for support
is the tailored experience.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:31.959
In the Google Group
forums or Stack Overflow,

00:13:31.959 --> 00:13:33.500
the answers to the
questions is going

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.410
to be generic and try
to help facilitate

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:39.340
a conversation for a
wide variety of audience,

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:40.840
people coming to it later.

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:44.640
In the paid support, you get
tailored one-to-one answers

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.840
that address your
very specific needs

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:49.310
and usually involve
exactly what you're doing.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:50.060
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:54.540
And I say also in the gold
level it's not just hey,

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:55.390
I've got a problem.

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.020
But I believe it's also like
I'm trying to find the best

00:13:58.020 --> 00:13:58.820
way to do this.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.350
Can you help me work that out?

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:02.150
Is that true?

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:03.150
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:06.220
That's something we do
actually quite regularly.

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:07.750
And I have some
really great stories

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:09.540
about things along those lines.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:10.270
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh, let's hear it.

00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:10.606
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:10.606 --> 00:14:11.280
Let's hear them.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:12.279
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:14:12.279 --> 00:14:16.500
So one of them--
we had a customer.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.010
they're a really
big video game now.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.410
But before they got really
big they called us up

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:24.520
and they're like, hey,
we have a problem.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:25.630
And we sat there.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:26.480
And we helped them.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.970
And we actually had two
different support reps

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:33.230
sit on the phone with them
for eight hours one day,

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:34.940
eight hours the next
day, and then four

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:36.950
hours on the third day.

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:38.360
And we actually helped it.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:41.120
It was actually quite
interesting to watch

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.750
to see like the support
rep and the customer--

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:44.950
they'd have conversations.

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:46.658
And they'd be agreed,
be like, all right.

00:14:46.658 --> 00:14:47.750
Let's go grab a snack.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:49.375
We'll come back in
15 minutes and we'll

00:14:49.375 --> 00:14:50.640
continue the conversation.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.181
These are kind of
the types of things

00:14:52.181 --> 00:14:53.310
that we don't mind doing.

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:55.690
And it's part of that
like additional help.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:00.915
And really, they were having
some issues around Cloud SQL.

00:15:00.915 --> 00:15:02.790
And we were helping them
get past the growing

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:06.140
pains that some customers
experience with Cloud SQL.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:07.110
MARK MANDEL: Brilliant.

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.760
So it sounds like it's really
great, not just the hey,

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.520
I've got a problem, but I
haven't used this platform

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.030
before, and I have built
this particular thing before.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.670
And it allows you to kind
of have that sanity check

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.860
or like that extra
pair of eyes to be

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.110
able to see am I not
doing something completely

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.182
and utterly crazy.

00:15:23.182 --> 00:15:24.182
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:15:24.182 --> 00:15:28.870
Most of the support reps
generally pass a full Google

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:30.690
engineering interview.

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:33.240
And so we all have experience
with distributed systems

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:34.600
and scaling systems.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:40.600
And so actually, where you get
the best benefit from the gold

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.836
and platinum support
is actually people

00:15:42.836 --> 00:15:43.960
who have done these things.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:47.084
And so in this case
with this past customer

00:15:47.084 --> 00:15:48.250
they had a proof of concept.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:49.500
It was running on Google SQL.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:52.000
And then they tried to throw
like 200 times load at it.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:53.940
And they're like, it fell over.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:55.470
And so we're helping
them facilitate

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.690
through those types of things.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.740
BRIAN DORSEY: What's the
solution there because I

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:00.855
want this to scale up.

00:16:00.855 --> 00:16:03.420
I'm going to get 200
times on my project too.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:04.300
What's the answer.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:05.716
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
Sure, you will.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:12.900
So actually this was before
Cloud SQL was-- or, Cloud

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.660
SQL V2 was a big thing.

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:18.530
And so we actually helped them
install, and configure, and low

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:21.510
test my SQL running on GCE.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:23.030
And that helped him
out quite a bit.

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:23.650
BRIAN DORSEY: And
if someone were

00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:25.510
running with a similar
situation today, what

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:26.676
would the recommendation be?

00:16:26.676 --> 00:16:28.070
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Cloud SQL V2.

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:28.850
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:29.860
It's that much faster?

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:30.860
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:35.635
So Cloud SQL V1 has a
couple of limitations,

00:16:35.635 --> 00:16:37.510
like the number of
connections you could have

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:38.720
or how big it is.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:40.820
And the way this
customer was going,

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:43.280
they needed a lot more power.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:47.790
And so Cloud SQL V2 wasn't
quite ready, even for beta

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:48.810
at the time.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.670
And so really, I would
recommend it now.

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.760
It's many more connections,
much bigger instances.

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:56.395
It's pretty amazing.

00:16:56.395 --> 00:16:57.270
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:00.446
So that sounds like a
pretty specific case.

00:17:00.446 --> 00:17:02.320
Are there any common
things that people often

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.310
come to support for, things
you often see quite regularly?

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:06.670
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:08.470
There's a couple things.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:12.260
Most of them actually come
from either GCE or Cloud SQL.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:14.760
There seems to be a trend here.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.825
So connection
limits in Cloud SQL

00:17:16.825 --> 00:17:19.290
is something that
happens all the time.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.450
Something else that
happens from Cloud SQL

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:25.770
is sometimes we have to perform
maintenance on your instances.

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:28.770
And so sometimes
they get rebooted

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.030
as part of the maintenance.

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:34.030
But customers like to enable
a flag called the general log

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:35.980
on their Cloud SQL instances.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.810
And so even though we've
rebooted your instance,

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:42.880
and it was only down for 15
seconds as we rebooted it,

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:46.350
sometimes it can take up to
five hours for your Cloud SQL

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.930
instance to recover
because it's trying

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:50.383
to go through the general log.

00:17:50.383 --> 00:17:51.133
BRIAN DORSEY: Wow.

00:17:51.133 --> 00:17:51.526
OK.

00:17:51.526 --> 00:17:52.025
Wait.

00:17:52.025 --> 00:17:55.254
How do we avoid this on my thing
that's going to go to 200x?

00:17:55.254 --> 00:17:56.670
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
So Cloud SQL V2

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:58.920
allows you to add
your own My SQL flags.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:01.470
So just don't add the enable
general log My SQL flag.

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:02.595
BRIAN DORSEY: And we're OK?

00:18:02.595 --> 00:18:02.880
OK.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:03.640
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.790
But that's something
that happens quite often.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.970
So the general log is
really good for debugging.

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:11.490
But people forget
to disable it when

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.510
they go live into production.

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:18.630
And so they get bitten by
this, most around 30 minutes.

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.614
But and then customers
try to reboot there

00:18:21.614 --> 00:18:23.530
because you can do that
once it comes back up.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.540
You can reboot your
Cloud SQL instance.

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:27.950
And so they only actually
cause themselves more pain when

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:29.670
they click the reboot button.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:30.620
BRIAN DORSEY: It just
takes that much longer.

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:30.830
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:32.560
They'll get 15
minutes in and reboot.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.824
So really, you just
got to be patient.

00:18:34.824 --> 00:18:35.490
You got to wait.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:38.320
There's nothing we can
do for your instance.

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.910
You just got to wait.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.882
So disable your gentle logs.

00:18:41.882 --> 00:18:43.090
Make sure those are disabled.

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:43.320
BRIAN DORSEY: Got you.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:44.460
Lesson for the day.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:44.670
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:47.128
Rule number one in Cloud SQL,
don't enable the general log.

00:18:47.128 --> 00:18:49.520
MARK MANDEL: That's a
great lesson for the day.

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:52.100
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
On GCE some of things

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:56.040
that sometimes catch customers
is the log migrations.

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:58.650
We try to make them as
painless as possible.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:01.540
But there are some pieces
of software out there

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:07.320
that can't handle five
seconds of the network

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:08.820
not being available.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:11.290
So distributed
clustered systems,

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:14.720
like sub-millisecond or
sub-second heartbeats.

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:18.210
And if you fail two of
them, your node's dead.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.110
And so we've handled that a
couple of times and being like,

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:22.290
yeah.

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:26.360
Live migration sometimes
can take one or two seconds.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:30.510
And your software's health beats
are a little too aggressive.

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:35.150
So if you can turn
them down, you

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:38.340
won't have to do manual recovery
of your [INAUDIBLE] system

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:40.296
every time there's
a lot of migration.

00:19:40.296 --> 00:19:43.870
So those are the common
ones that I-- well,

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.260
another common one is
actually firewall rules.

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.740
Those trip people
up all the time.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:50.230
I was actually
handling one today

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:54.020
where some customers really
like to limit their firewall

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:57.220
rules to be their
office's IP address.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:59.850
But very few people actually
have a static IP address.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:03.290
And so when their office, the
IP address of their office

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:06.822
changes, they're like, why
can't I SSH into my instances?

00:20:06.822 --> 00:20:08.280
The IP address
you're trying to log

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.650
in as different than your
firewall rules allow.

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:13.420
So you have to coach customers
through those sorts of things.

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:14.295
BRIAN DORSEY: Gotcha.

00:20:14.295 --> 00:20:16.770
So I'm on a cable connection
or something in my office.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.070
And its on [INAUDIBLE] DNS.

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:19.460
And one day it just changes.

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:20.460
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.780
Sometimes I see a
customer's firewall rules.

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:25.350
And it's Brian's
home IP address.

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:26.610
And I can't log in from home.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:28.250
And it's like, yep.

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.180
Looks like your IP
address changed.

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:32.680
BRIAN DORSEY: So can you just
look at these for customers?

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:33.440
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Look at what?

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:34.710
BRIAN DORSEY: Like,
my firewall rules.

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:35.670
Can use look at that?

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:36.780
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:37.280
I can--

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:38.405
BRIAN DORSEY: Wait, really?

00:20:40.259 --> 00:20:42.050
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: With
restrictions, yeah.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:42.780
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:43.630
How does that work?

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:45.340
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: So first
off, you need to tell me

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:46.560
that there's a problem.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:47.269
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:20:47.269 --> 00:20:49.643
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: And then
as with everything at Google,

00:20:49.643 --> 00:20:52.250
everything is logged and you
need a reason to do anything.

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:57.410
So when you submit a case, I
can look at the metainformation

00:20:57.410 --> 00:20:59.970
of your project, so
which firewalls you have

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:01.360
and which instances you have.

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:03.410
I can't actually log
into any of them.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.540
But I can see the name
of your instances.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:09.410
So if you're like oh, my
web server 01 is acting up,

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:12.090
I can actually go and see which
instance in your project is

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:15.020
that and then be able to
inspect information about it,

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:18.870
like how much CPU it's
using, how much disc and RAM

00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:19.370
it's using.

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:20.078
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:21:20.078 --> 00:21:22.120
As part of requesting
support, but I'm

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:24.500
kind of asking you to be able
to take a look at metadata

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:25.291
around the project.

00:21:25.291 --> 00:21:26.809
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yes.

00:21:26.809 --> 00:21:28.350
It would be really
hard to do our job

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:30.570
without being able to do that.

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:35.400
Or, the alternative is
we play the game of can

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.280
you run this G-Cloud command
and send me he output.

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:40.180
And so that is a possibility.

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:42.690
But it's not the
most efficient way

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:44.850
for us to help you
solve your problem.

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:48.366
And when you're already anxious
and you're down in production,

00:21:48.366 --> 00:21:49.740
you'd rather we
just tell you how

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:54.226
to solve the problem then ask
you to run 15 commands for us.

00:21:54.226 --> 00:21:55.100
BRIAN DORSEY: Gotcha.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:56.683
MARK MANDEL: That
make a lot of sense.

00:21:56.683 --> 00:21:59.570
I wanted to return a little
bit to the different tiers

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:02.440
in support so that
people can have

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:05.280
a feel for what tier they
might want to use depending

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.250
on where they're at with their
Google Cloud Platform usage.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:10.500
Bronze seems very obvious
because you're already

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:11.399
signed in.

00:22:11.399 --> 00:22:13.690
We talk a little bit about
silver I think to the degree

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:14.565
that you're like, OK.

00:22:14.565 --> 00:22:16.130
You get the
personalized experience.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:17.790
For me, looking
at gold, it seems

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:21.320
like the big win there is you
get that consultative ticket.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.070
So you get that ability
to be able to ask,

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.710
give me a guide on how
to build this thing.

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:27.509
I've never done it before.

00:22:27.509 --> 00:22:29.300
At what point do you
think people should be

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:31.200
moving to from silver to gold?

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.460
Is it just because
they have that need?

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.250
And then also, what
point do you think

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:37.790
people should be moving
from gold to platinum?

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:40.530
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
So the big thing,

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:42.600
I think, changing
from silver to gold,

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:45.000
is when you need
that 24 by 7 support.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:48.050
That's really the big difference
between silver and gold,

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:50.450
is when your stuff is
down on the weekend

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:52.950
and you need someone to
answer your questions

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:56.940
so your business is back up and
running, gold is the right one.

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:59.400
MARK MANDEL: And specifically
that's phone support as well?

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:01.470
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah,
phone support as well.

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:05.340
And so even if you look
at the target response

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:08.240
times on things-- so when
you're down in production,

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:10.520
I would call that a P1
ticket, like priority one.

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:11.100
I'm down.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:12.150
I'm losing money.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.240
We target to respond
within one hour.

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.930
When you're on the silver
level, the target to response

00:23:17.930 --> 00:23:18.945
is four business hours.

00:23:18.945 --> 00:23:20.320
So if you're down
on the weekend,

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:24.490
it could be up till 1 o'clock
Australian time on a Monday

00:23:24.490 --> 00:23:26.400
when we would start
responding to you.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:27.850
So if you're on
the Pacific time,

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:31.940
it would be about 9
o'clock PM Sunday.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:36.559
And then from gold to silver,
or from gold to platinum,

00:23:36.559 --> 00:23:37.850
it's only an extension of that.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:40.670
So we target responses
in 15 minutes.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.920
So if you're a
really big customer,

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:45.960
and you lose thousands,
tens of thousands,

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:49.080
millions per a minute down,
platinum's the place to be.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:50.330
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:52.496
BRIAN DORSEY: And it looks
like the other difference

00:23:52.496 --> 00:23:54.788
there is this critical impact
issues versus high impact

00:23:54.788 --> 00:23:55.287
issues.

00:23:55.287 --> 00:23:56.590
What's the difference there?

00:23:56.590 --> 00:23:58.131
TERRANCE SHEPHERD:
So critical impact

00:23:58.131 --> 00:24:00.320
is system is down,
in production,

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.030
and I'm losing money.

00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:05.700
High impact, it's
hard to describe.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:08.417
But things just short
of that are high impact.

00:24:08.417 --> 00:24:10.000
It's always hard to
just-- so it would

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:11.820
be classified as priority.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:17.065
So P1s are like systems down
and losing money in production.

00:24:17.065 --> 00:24:19.060
P2, it would be high impact.

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:22.270
It's always hard to
describe what a P2 is.

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:25.842
So we support you on
weekends with P2 stuff.

00:24:25.842 --> 00:24:26.550
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:28.383
So I notice the disk
is filling or some sort

00:24:28.383 --> 00:24:30.884
of like performance degradation.

00:24:30.884 --> 00:24:33.300
Things are trending towards
bad, but they haven't actually

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:33.820
failed yet.

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:35.630
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:39.670
Not that it happens often,
but let's say for example

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:42.380
you're putting a lot
of objects into GCS.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:48.080
And GCS, as example, GCS
has an error rate of 1%.

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:51.840
And you start seeing GCS
go to 2%, 3%, 4%, or 5%.

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:56.380
That would be a time to file
a P2 ticket on platinum,

00:24:56.380 --> 00:24:59.440
well, if you had
platinum, and tell us hey.

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:01.725
GSC error rates are going up.

00:25:01.725 --> 00:25:03.850
Our engineering team should
already be aware of it.

00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:05.840
But that would be
a P2 thing of we're

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.330
kind of going towards
the bad situation.

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:10.980
Can you identify and solve
it before we are actually

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:12.020
down in production?

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:12.720
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:13.110
Brilliant.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:13.480
OK.

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:15.771
So we're starting to run out
of time just a little bit.

00:25:15.771 --> 00:25:17.710
But all the support stuff
sounds really great.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.330
So how do I sign up?

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:20.880
What's the best
way to get started?

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:24.150
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: So you can
sign up for silver and gold

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:27.395
just by clicking the buttons
on cloud.google.com/support.

00:25:27.395 --> 00:25:29.420
It will actually tell
you how to do it.

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:33.610
For platinum support, it
requires a little more work

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:35.510
and a little more effort.

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:37.360
Usually involves a
sales team member

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:41.430
to actually start the process
in an offline contract,

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:42.740
not necessarily strictly there.

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:44.870
But that's generally
how the process goes.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:45.090
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:46.580
But I can access
buttons for that

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:48.790
to get in contact through
that same URL I believe.

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:49.790
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Yeah.

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:50.560
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:51.559
BRIAN DORSEY: All right.

00:25:51.559 --> 00:25:53.525
And is there anything
else about support

00:25:53.525 --> 00:25:54.900
that we haven't
covered, or you'd

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:58.489
want to say, or plug, or
anything extra you want to add?

00:25:58.489 --> 00:26:00.780
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Probably
only thing to add is that--

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:02.300
or to reiterate--
is that the people

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:06.340
you're talking on the other end
are basically Google engineers.

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:10.660
It's the same people that
comes out of same talent

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:13.680
pool as [INAUDIBLE] at Google.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:16.040
And they just have
some extra skills

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.192
that we think make them
good to be in this position.

00:26:19.192 --> 00:26:20.400
BRIAN DORSEY: That's awesome.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:21.941
MARK MANDEL: That
is very, very cool.

00:26:21.941 --> 00:26:22.630
All right.

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:24.720
Well, Terrance and
Brian, thank you

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:28.250
so much for joining me today and
joining us here on the Google

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:29.980
Cloud Platform podcast.

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:31.790
That was a super
interesting conversation.

00:26:31.790 --> 00:26:33.637
TERRANCE SHEPHERD: Thanks.

00:26:33.637 --> 00:26:35.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
thank you so much,

