I'm looking forward to that.

00:27:13.036 --> 00:27:13.535
Cool.

00:27:13.535 --> 00:27:15.590
So let's go with the
question of the week.

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:16.590
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.330
So we're talking about
this a little before.

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:20.997
Basically, it runs
along the lines

00:27:20.997 --> 00:27:23.330
of-- say you have your app
running on something like App

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:27.700
Engine and you want to connect
to one of the other Google

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:31.430
services that run in the
cloud such as Calendar,

00:27:31.430 --> 00:27:33.277
or possibly Drive.

00:27:33.277 --> 00:27:34.860
I've had this question
before as well.

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:36.060
I have no idea how to do it.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:37.514
But I do believe
you do, Fransesc.

00:27:37.514 --> 00:27:38.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:41.150
And I actually got
this same question

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:42.982
but in a slightly different way.

00:27:42.982 --> 00:27:44.440
And I'm going to
share it because I

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:45.565
thought it was really cool.

00:27:45.565 --> 00:27:48.830
There was these people that
were doing a little application

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:51.140
where you could take a picture.

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:54.140
This picture could be sent
to the Cloud Vision API.

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:58.970
They could do OCR, so character
recognition on that image,

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:03.480
and then create a new
document on Google Docs

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:05.552
we text recognize
from the image, which

00:28:05.552 --> 00:28:06.760
I thought it was very useful.

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:07.170
MARK MANDEL: That's cool.

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:08.211
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah.

00:28:08.211 --> 00:28:09.980
So the [INAUDIBLE] is like-- OK.

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:13.220
So Google Cloud Vision API--
obviously. you used the API.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:17.070
How do you do the
docs part of it.

00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:19.470
And there's actually a
couple ways of doing it.

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:24.845
But the easiest one is using
the simply the REST API.

00:28:24.845 --> 00:28:28.900
Most Google services,
they provide an API.

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:30.600
And it's mostly REST APIs.

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:32.270
There's also sometimes
the GRPC APIs.

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:36.380
But you're going to see REST
APIs, which means that you

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:38.130
can use them from anywhere.

00:28:38.130 --> 00:28:40.790
You can use them very
easily from your own machine

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:42.640
if you want to try them out.

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:44.940
There's also something
that I think that people

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:46.150
should know about if.

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:49.860
If you have never tried it,
it's Google API's Explorer,

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:52.130
which is actually basically
a playground where you

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:54.740
can see all the APIs we offer.

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:57.550
And then you can directly
form there just fill the gaps,

00:28:57.550 --> 00:28:59.315
like fill the fields
that you want to try.

00:28:59.315 --> 00:29:00.190
And they just run it.

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:03.440
And it manages all the
authentication part of it.

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:04.600
So it's very nice.

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:07.060
But obviously, you
can also do that

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:10.720
from any language
running anywhere.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:13.820
So you can do it directly
from the compute engine,

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:15.266
from container engine.

00:29:15.266 --> 00:29:16.890
And if you're doing
it from App Engine,

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:19.440
you're going to need to use
things like QRL Fetch, which

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:24.820
allows you to do ATP requests
from an App Engine instance

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:26.030
to the outside world.

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:26.780
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:29.310
Now, do I need to build
those REST requests myself,

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:31.560
are there any SDKs
or client tools

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:33.770
that I could use to make my
life a little bit easier?

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:34.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:35.238
That is a very good question.

00:29:35.238 --> 00:29:37.340
So if you are the
kind of person that

00:29:37.340 --> 00:29:39.890
wants to create everything
by himself, by themselves,

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:41.050
it's totally fine.

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:42.510
You can totally do that.

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:45.190
But we also offer a
bunch of libraries

00:29:45.190 --> 00:29:49.890
that basically offer
the same functionality,

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:51.710
but an idiomatic way.

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:55.020
So for instance,
for Google Docs,

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:59.673
you can see that there is Java,
JavaScript.net, Objective C,

00:29:59.673 --> 00:30:07.290
PHP, Python, and then also DART
and GO, obviously, and Node.js,

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:07.880
Ruby.

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:09.879
And I'm pretty sure there's
even more than that.

00:30:09.879 --> 00:30:11.520
But basically, all
of those libraries

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:15.590
are developed
sometimes by engineers.

00:30:15.590 --> 00:30:19.580
So basically, they're
going to be very idiomatic.

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:21.864
And they're going to feel
like normal libraries.

00:30:21.864 --> 00:30:24.530
Sometimes for other services you
will see that they are actually

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:26.320
autogenerated.

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:27.900
So they will not
be as idiomatic.

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.620
But still, it's
easier than writing

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:31.636
the whole requests yourself.

00:30:31.636 --> 00:30:33.260
MARK MANDEL: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:34.301
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah.

00:30:34.301 --> 00:30:35.390
So you can check it out.

00:30:35.390 --> 00:30:37.640
We'll have the links
in the show notes.

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:41.860
And that way you can easy
integrate Google Cloud

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:44.489
